Reliable Data Transmission with Parity Check

This project demonstrates reliable serial data transmission using an Arduino Nano, separate clock and data lines, a parity bit for basic error detection, and a 16×2 LCD display for real-time visualization.

Features
-Character-by-character data transmission
-Bit-by-bit communication using dedicated clock and data signals
-LCD display showing transmitted characters and binary data
-Parity bit generation and transmission
-Basic error detection support
-Adjustable transmission rate

Working Principle
The message "Hello, world!" is loaded into the transmitter.
Each character is converted into its 8-bit binary representation.
Bits are transmitted one at a time through the data line.
A clock pulse synchronizes the sender and receiver for every bit.
The LCD displays:
The original message on the first row.
The binary representation of the currently transmitted character on the second row.
After all characters are sent, a parity bit is transmitted.
The parity bit can be used by the receiver to detect transmission errors.

Concepts Learned
-Digital Communication Systems
-Synchronous Data Transmission
-Clock and Data Signal Separation
-Binary Data Representation
-Parity-Based Error Detection
-LCD Interfacing with Arduino
-Embedded Systems Programming
-Transmission Format
[8 Data Bits] → [Parity Bit]

Example:

Character: A
Binary:    01000001
Parity:    0 (Even Parity)

